IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `pa-tort-dates`

-- Privacy Act 1988 (Cth) -- the questions of the person whose privacy was invaded and who wants a remedy: the statutory
-- tort for serious invasions of privacy (Schedule 2), the representative complaint and the Commissioner's determination
-- (Part V), and the court's compensation orders for credit reporting contraventions (Part IIIA Division 7).
--
-- SEAT: the individual whose privacy was invaded: the plaintiff under Schedule 2; the complainant, or a class member of
-- a representative complaint, under Part V; the person who suffered loss from a credit reporting contravention under
-- ss 25 and 25A. "Can I sue?", "Is the person who did it exempt?", "Do they have a defence?", "By when must I start?",
-- "What can I be awarded, and is there a cap?", "Can I complain for a group, or leave one?", "What can the Commissioner
-- give me, and can I enforce it?". The court, the Commissioner and the Administrative Review Tribunal get no seat:
-- their findings and opinions are asked as facts.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts (yes/no, a number, a date, a choice from a list). No question takes a
-- DEONTIC or an EVENT. The duties with deadlines the person meets are regulative rules run inside questions: Schedule 2
-- cl 14 (commence proceedings before the cl 14(1) day, or before the day in the court's order), s 52A(1)(b) (the
-- respondent gives me a copy of the statement within 14 days, or the longer period allowed) and ss 25(2)(b), 25A(4)(b)
-- (apply within 6 years of the day the cause of action accrued).
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: Schedule 2 Part 2 (cl 7(1), (2); cl 6(1) "intruding upon the seclusion" and "misusing information";
-- cl 8; cl 10; cl 11; cl 12; cl 13; cl 14), Part 3 (cl 15 with the definitions in (2), (3); cls 16, 16A, 16B, 17, 18), Part 4
-- (cl 19; cl 20(1)); Part V ss 36(1)-(3), (5), 38, 38B(2), 39, 52(1)(b), (1AB), (1B), (3), (4), 52A(1)(b), 55A(1), (5), 62;
-- Part IIIA ss 25 and 25A. Out, with reasons, in NOTES.md (the APPs and Part IIIC are in the baseline of October 2026
-- beside this folder).
--
-- Edition: Privacy Act 1988, Compilation No. 104, compilation date 4 June 2026 (includes amendments to Act No. 75, 2025;
-- register id C2026C00227), deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A03712.txt (retrieved 13 October 2026).
-- Schedule 2 commenced on 10 June 2025 (endnote 3, Privacy and Other Legislation Amendment Act 2024 s 2(1) item 8); the
-- amending Act's application provision is not deposited (NOTES.md, finding 1).
--
-- Sourced from the Federal Register of Legislation, Commonwealth of Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Judgments the Schedule hands to the court ("reasonable expectation of
-- privacy", "serious", "the public interest ... outweighed", "exceptional circumstances", "reasonably believed",
-- "proportionate, necessary and reasonable", "satisfied that it was not reasonable") are INPUTS: the answer is
-- conditional on them. Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05 (NOTES.md): an answer that is
-- the text's own no is marked "(the text's no)"; an answer where the text is limited by its own wording or silent is
-- marked "NOTHING-kind".

§ `Privacy Act 1988: the questions of the person whose privacy was invaded`

§§ `Schedule 2 cl 7 -- Do I have a cause of action for a serious invasion of privacy?`

-- cl 7(1): "An individual (the plaintiff) has a cause of action in tort against another person (the defendant) if:
-- (a) the defendant invaded the plaintiff's privacy by doing one or both of the following: (i) intruding upon the
-- plaintiff's seclusion; (ii) misusing information that relates to the plaintiff; and (b) a person in the position of
-- the plaintiff would have had a reasonable expectation of privacy in all of the circumstances; and (c) the invasion of
-- privacy was intentional or reckless; and (d) the invasion of privacy was serious; and (e) the public interest in the
-- plaintiff's privacy outweighed any countervailing public interest."
-- (2): "The invasion of privacy is actionable without proof of damage."
-- cl 6(1): "intruding upon the seclusion of an individual includes, but is not limited to, the following: (a) physically
-- intruding into the person's private space; (b) watching, listening to or recording the person's private activities or
-- private affairs." "misusing information that relates to an individual includes, but is not limited to, collecting,
-- using or disclosing information about the individual." Both lists are open ("includes, but is not limited to"): when
-- none of the listed acts happened and the court has not found another intrusion or misuse, the answer is NOTHING-kind.
-- cl 7(7): if the invasion is by misusing information, "it is immaterial whether the information was true" (no input).
-- cl 7(3), (5), (6) list matters the court may consider for (b), (d) and (e); the findings themselves are inputs.
-- "reckless has the same meaning as in the Criminal Code" (cl 6(1)): borrowed, asked as a fact.

GIVEN `physically intruded` IS A BOOLEAN, `watched listened or recorded` IS A BOOLEAN, `collected used or disclosed` IS A BOOLEAN, `court finds another intrusion or misuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`cl 7(1)(a) -- did they invade my privacy` `physically intruded` `watched listened or recorded` `collected used or disclosed` `court finds another intrusion or misuse` MEANS
    IF `physically intruded` OR `watched listened or recorded` OR `collected used or disclosed` OR `court finds another intrusion or misuse`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `Is there a cause of action` IS ONE OF
    `yes: all five elements of cl 7(1) are met; the invasion is actionable without proof of damage (cl 7(2))`
    `not shown: the Schedule's lists of intrusions and misuses are open, and none of the listed acts is said to have happened; whether what happened intruded upon your seclusion or misused information about you is for the court (cl 6(1), cl 7(1)(a), NOTHING-kind)`
    `no: a person in your position would not have had a reasonable expectation of privacy in all the circumstances (cl 7(1)(b), the text's no)`
    `no: the invasion was not intentional or reckless (cl 7(1)(c), the text's no)`
    `no: the invasion was not serious (cl 7(1)(d), the text's no)`
    `no: the public interest in your privacy did not outweigh the countervailing public interest (cl 7(1)(e), the text's no)`

@export Do I have a cause of action in tort for a serious invasion of privacy (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 7)? There must be an intrusion upon my seclusion or a misuse of information about me, a reasonable expectation of privacy, intention or recklessness, seriousness, and the public interest in my privacy must outweigh any countervailing public interest. No damage need be proved.
GIVEN `physically intruded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person physically intruded into your private space
      `watched listened or recorded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person watched, listened to or recorded your private activities or private affairs
      `collected used or disclosed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person collected, used or disclosed information about you
      `court finds another intrusion or misuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that something else the person did intruded upon your seclusion or misused information about you (answer no if not yet decided)
      `reasonable expectation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that a person in your position would have had a reasonable expectation of privacy in all the circumstances
      `intentional or reckless` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion was intentional or reckless (reckless as in the Criminal Code: aware of a substantial risk and unjustifiably taking it)
      `serious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the invasion of privacy was serious (for example by the offence, distress or harm to dignity it was likely to cause)
      `public interest outweighs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the public interest in your privacy outweighed any countervailing public interest (such as freedom of expression or of the media, open justice, public health and safety, or the prevention of crime)
GIVETH AN `Is there a cause of action`
`cl 7 -- do I have a cause of action` `physically intruded` `watched listened or recorded` `collected used or disclosed` `court finds another intrusion or misuse` `reasonable expectation` `intentional or reckless` `serious` `public interest outweighs` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`the invasion` EQUALS JUST TRUE))
        THEN `not shown: the Schedule's lists of intrusions and misuses are open, and none of the listed acts is said to have happened; whether what happened intruded upon your seclusion or misused information about you is for the court (cl 6(1), cl 7(1)(a), NOTHING-kind)`
        IF (NOT `reasonable expectation`)
        THEN `no: a person in your position would not have had a reasonable expectation of privacy in all the circumstances (cl 7(1)(b), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `intentional or reckless`)
        THEN `no: the invasion was not intentional or reckless (cl 7(1)(c), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `serious`)
        THEN `no: the invasion was not serious (cl 7(1)(d), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `public interest outweighs`)
        THEN `no: the public interest in your privacy did not outweigh the countervailing public interest (cl 7(1)(e), the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: all five elements of cl 7(1) are met; the invasion is actionable without proof of damage (cl 7(2))`
    WHERE
        `the invasion` MEANS `cl 7(1)(a) -- did they invade my privacy` `physically intruded` `watched listened or recorded` `collected used or disclosed` `court finds another intrusion or misuse`

§§ `Schedule 2 Part 3 -- Is the person who invaded my privacy exempt?`

-- cl 15(1): "This Schedule does not apply to an invasion of privacy by any of the following to the extent that the
-- invasion of privacy involves the collection, preparation for publication or publication of journalistic material:
-- (a) a journalist; (b) an employer of, or a person engaging, a journalist; (c) a person assisting a journalist who is
-- employed or engaged by: (i) the journalist's employer; or (ii) a person engaging the journalist; (d) a person
-- assisting a journalist in the person's professional capacity."
-- (1A): "This Schedule does not apply to an invasion of privacy to the extent that the invasion of privacy involves the
-- publication or distribution of journalistic material that was prepared for publication by a journalist."
-- (2): "A journalist is a person who: (a) works in a professional capacity as a journalist; and (b) is subject to:
-- (i) standards of professional conduct that apply to journalists; or (ii) a code of practice that applies to
-- journalists." (3): "Material is journalistic material if it: (a) has the character of news, current affairs or a
-- documentary; or (b) consists of commentary or opinion on, or analysis of, news, current affairs or a documentary; or
-- (c) consists of editorial content relating to news, current affairs or a documentary." (4): breach of the standards
-- or code is immaterial (no input).
-- cl 16: "This Schedule does not apply to an invasion of an individual's privacy by an agency or a State or Territory
-- authority (other than an intelligence agency or a law enforcement body) to the extent that the agency or authority
-- invades the individual's privacy, in good faith: (a) in the performance or purported performance of a function of the
-- agency or authority; or (b) in the exercise or purported exercise of a power of the agency or authority."
-- cl 16A: the same for "a staff member of an agency or a State or Territory authority (other than a staff member of an
-- intelligence agency or a law enforcement body)".
-- cl 16B: "This Schedule does not apply to: (a) an invasion of privacy by a law enforcement body; or (b) an invasion of
-- privacy by a person who is a staff member of a law enforcement body in the performance of the person's duties, powers
-- or functions as such a staff member; or (c) an invasion of privacy to the extent that it involves a disclosure of
-- information to a law enforcement body; or (d) an invasion of privacy to the extent that it involves information that
-- was disclosed by a law enforcement body."
-- cl 17: the same for an intelligence agency, with (aa) "a person who is an ASIO affiliate, or an agent or staff member
-- of an intelligence agency, in the performance of" its functions or duties.
-- cl 18: "This Schedule does not apply to an invasion of privacy by a person who is under 18 years of age."
-- Each is a flat negative ("does not apply"): where one holds, the answer is the text's no. "To the extent that" is
-- answered for the invasion as a whole (fork F4). The lists in cl 6(1) of law enforcement bodies and intelligence
-- agencies are closed ("means"); the person says which kind of body acted.

DECLARE `Who invaded my privacy` IS ONE OF
    `a person or business not listed below`
    `a journalist`
    `the employer of a journalist, or a person engaging a journalist`
    `a person assisting a journalist, employed or engaged by the journalist's employer or by the person engaging the journalist`
    `a person assisting a journalist in that person's professional capacity`
    `a Commonwealth agency, or a State or Territory authority, that is not an intelligence agency or law enforcement body`
    `a staff member of such an agency or authority`
    `a law enforcement body`
    `a staff member of a law enforcement body`
    `an intelligence agency`
    `an ASIO affiliate, or an agent or staff member of an intelligence agency`

DECLARE `What the material was` IS ONE OF
    `news, current affairs or a documentary`
    `commentary or opinion on, or analysis of, news, current affairs or a documentary`
    `editorial content relating to news, current affairs or a documentary`
    `none of these`

GIVEN `the material` IS A `What the material was`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `cl 15(3) -- is it journalistic material` `the material` IF
    CONSIDER `the material`
    WHEN `none of these` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `works professionally as a journalist` IS A BOOLEAN, `subject to journalists' standards or code` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `cl 15(2) -- is the journalist a journalist` `works professionally as a journalist` `subject to journalists' standards or code` IF
    `works professionally as a journalist` AND `subject to journalists' standards or code`

GIVEN `who` IS A `Who invaded my privacy`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `cl 15(1) -- is in the journalist list` `who` IF
    CONSIDER `who`
    WHEN `a journalist` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the employer of a journalist, or a person engaging a journalist` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a person assisting a journalist, employed or engaged by the journalist's employer or by the person engaging the journalist` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a person assisting a journalist in that person's professional capacity` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Is the invasion exempt` IS ONE OF
    `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to a journalist, a journalist's employer or engager, or a person assisting a journalist, collecting, preparing for publication or publishing journalistic material (cl 15(1), the text's no)`
    `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to publishing or distributing journalistic material that a journalist prepared for publication (cl 15(1A), the text's no)`
    `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to an agency or State or Territory authority acting in good faith in its functions or powers (cl 16, the text's no)`
    `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to a staff member of an agency or State or Territory authority acting in good faith in its functions or powers (cl 16A, the text's no)`
    `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to a law enforcement body, or its staff acting in their duties, powers or functions (cl 16B(a), (b), the text's no)`
    `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to an intelligence agency, or an ASIO affiliate or an agent or staff member of one acting in their functions or duties (cl 17(a), (aa), the text's no)`
    `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to an invasion involving a disclosure of information to, or information disclosed by, a law enforcement body or an intelligence agency (cl 16B(c), (d), cl 17(b), (c), the text's no)`
    `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to an invasion of privacy by a person under 18 (cl 18, the text's no)`
    `no exemption in Part 3 of Schedule 2 applies on these facts`

@export Is the person or body who invaded my privacy exempt from the statutory tort (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 Part 3, cls 15-18)? The Schedule does not apply to journalists and those working with them on journalistic material, to agencies and authorities and their staff acting in good faith, to law enforcement and intelligence bodies and information passing to or from them, or to a person under 18.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who invaded my privacy` @desc Who invaded your privacy. One of: "a person or business not listed below", "a journalist", "the employer of a journalist, or a person engaging a journalist", "a person assisting a journalist, employed or engaged by the journalist's employer or by the person engaging the journalist", "a person assisting a journalist in that person's professional capacity", "a Commonwealth agency, or a State or Territory authority, that is not an intelligence agency or law enforcement body", "a staff member of such an agency or authority", "a law enforcement body", "a staff member of a law enforcement body", "an intelligence agency", "an ASIO affiliate, or an agent or staff member of an intelligence agency"
      `works professionally as a journalist` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where a journalist is involved: whether that journalist works in a professional capacity as a journalist
      `subject to journalists' standards or code` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where a journalist is involved: whether that journalist is subject to standards of professional conduct, or a code of practice, that apply to journalists
      `the material` IS A `What the material was` @desc What the material involved was. One of: "news, current affairs or a documentary", "commentary or opinion on, or analysis of, news, current affairs or a documentary", "editorial content relating to news, current affairs or a documentary", "none of these"
      `collecting preparing or publishing it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion involved collecting, preparing for publication or publishing that material
      `publishing or distributing a journalist's material` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion involved publishing or distributing that material after a journalist prepared it for publication
      `in good faith in a function or power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where an agency, authority or its staff member acted: whether it acted in good faith in performing (or purporting to perform) a function, or exercising (or purporting to exercise) a power, of the agency or authority
      `in the performance of duties or functions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where a staff member, agent or affiliate of a law enforcement body or intelligence agency acted: whether it was in the performance of their duties, powers or functions as such
      `disclosure to law enforcement or intelligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion involved disclosing information to a law enforcement body or an intelligence agency
      `information from law enforcement or intelligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion involved information that a law enforcement body or an intelligence agency had disclosed
      `the invader was under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who invaded your privacy was under 18 years of age
GIVETH AN `Is the invasion exempt`
`Part 3 -- is the invasion exempt` `who` `works professionally as a journalist` `subject to journalists' standards or code` `the material` `collecting preparing or publishing it` `publishing or distributing a journalist's material` `in good faith in a function or power` `in the performance of duties or functions` `disclosure to law enforcement or intelligence` `information from law enforcement or intelligence` `the invader was under 18` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `cl 15(1) -- is in the journalist list` `who` AND `there is a journalist` AND `it is journalistic material` AND `collecting preparing or publishing it`
        THEN `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to a journalist, a journalist's employer or engager, or a person assisting a journalist, collecting, preparing for publication or publishing journalistic material (cl 15(1), the text's no)`
        IF `there is a journalist` AND `it is journalistic material` AND `publishing or distributing a journalist's material`
        THEN `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to publishing or distributing journalistic material that a journalist prepared for publication (cl 15(1A), the text's no)`
        IF `who` EQUALS `a Commonwealth agency, or a State or Territory authority, that is not an intelligence agency or law enforcement body` AND `in good faith in a function or power`
        THEN `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to an agency or State or Territory authority acting in good faith in its functions or powers (cl 16, the text's no)`
        IF `who` EQUALS `a staff member of such an agency or authority` AND `in good faith in a function or power`
        THEN `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to a staff member of an agency or State or Territory authority acting in good faith in its functions or powers (cl 16A, the text's no)`
        IF `who` EQUALS `a law enforcement body`
            OR (`who` EQUALS `a staff member of a law enforcement body` AND `in the performance of duties or functions`)
        THEN `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to a law enforcement body, or its staff acting in their duties, powers or functions (cl 16B(a), (b), the text's no)`
        IF `who` EQUALS `an intelligence agency`
            OR (`who` EQUALS `an ASIO affiliate, or an agent or staff member of an intelligence agency` AND `in the performance of duties or functions`)
        THEN `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to an intelligence agency, or an ASIO affiliate or an agent or staff member of one acting in their functions or duties (cl 17(a), (aa), the text's no)`
        IF `disclosure to law enforcement or intelligence` OR `information from law enforcement or intelligence`
        THEN `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to an invasion involving a disclosure of information to, or information disclosed by, a law enforcement body or an intelligence agency (cl 16B(c), (d), cl 17(b), (c), the text's no)`
        IF `the invader was under 18`
        THEN `exempt: the Schedule does not apply to an invasion of privacy by a person under 18 (cl 18, the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `no exemption in Part 3 of Schedule 2 applies on these facts`
    WHERE
        `there is a journalist` MEANS `cl 15(2) -- is the journalist a journalist` `works professionally as a journalist` `subject to journalists' standards or code`
        `it is journalistic material` MEANS `cl 15(3) -- is it journalistic material` `the material`

§§ `Schedule 2 cl 8 -- Does the person who invaded my privacy have a defence?`

-- cl 8(1): "It is a defence to the cause of action if: (a) the invasion of privacy was required or authorised by or under
-- an Australian law or court/tribunal order; or (b) the plaintiff, or a person having lawful authority to do so for the
-- plaintiff, expressly or impliedly consented to the invasion of privacy; or (c) the defendant reasonably believed that
-- the invasion of privacy was necessary to prevent or lessen a serious threat to the life, health or safety of a
-- person; or (d) the invasion of privacy was: (i) incidental to the exercise of a lawful right of defence of persons or
-- property; and (ii) proportionate, necessary and reasonable."
-- (2): "It is also a defence to the cause of action if: (a) the defendant invaded the plaintiff's privacy by publishing,
-- within the meaning of an Australian law that deals with defamation, information that relates to the plaintiff; and
-- (b) the Australian law provides for a related defence; and (c) the defendant would be able to establish the related
-- defence if a reference in the Australian law to the publication of defamatory matter were to include a reference to
-- the invasion of privacy." (3): the related defences are absolute privilege, publication of public documents, and fair
-- report of proceedings of public concern.
-- "It is a defence ... if X": when no X is made out, this clause gives no defence on these facts (ruling (b) of
-- 2026-11-05), never "no defence at all". The defamation law is borrowed: (2)(b) and (c) are one input.

DECLARE `Does the defendant have a defence` IS ONE OF
    `defence: the invasion was required or authorised by or under an Australian law or a court or tribunal order (cl 8(1)(a))`
    `defence: you, or a person with lawful authority to do so for you, consented to it, expressly or impliedly (cl 8(1)(b))`
    `defence: the defendant reasonably believed it was necessary to prevent or lessen a serious threat to the life, health or safety of a person (cl 8(1)(c))`
    `defence: it was incidental to the exercise of a lawful right of defence of persons or property, and proportionate, necessary and reasonable (cl 8(1)(d))`
    `defence: it was a publication, and the defendant would establish a defamation-law defence of absolute privilege, publication of public documents, or fair report of proceedings of public concern (cl 8(2), (3))`
    `this clause gives no defence on these facts (cl 8, the text's no)`

@export Does the person who invaded my privacy have a defence to the statutory tort (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 8)? Defences are lawful authority or a court order, my consent, a reasonable belief that it was needed to prevent a serious threat to someone's life, health or safety, defence of persons or property, and three defamation-law defences for publications.
GIVEN `required or authorised by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion was required or authorised by or under an Australian law or a court or tribunal order
      `consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, or a person with lawful authority to do so for you, consented to the invasion, expressly or impliedly
      `reasonably believed necessary for safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant reasonably believed the invasion was necessary to prevent or lessen a serious threat to the life, health or safety of a person
      `incidental to defence of persons or property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion was incidental to the exercise of a lawful right of defence of persons or property
      `proportionate necessary and reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that invasion was proportionate, necessary and reasonable
      `was a publication under defamation law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion was publishing information about you, within the meaning of an Australian defamation law
      `would establish a related defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant would establish, under that defamation law, a defence of absolute privilege, of publication of public documents, or of fair report of proceedings of public concern
GIVETH A `Does the defendant have a defence`
`cl 8 -- does the defendant have a defence` `required or authorised by law` `consented` `reasonably believed necessary for safety` `incidental to defence of persons or property` `proportionate necessary and reasonable` `was a publication under defamation law` `would establish a related defence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `required or authorised by law`
        THEN `defence: the invasion was required or authorised by or under an Australian law or a court or tribunal order (cl 8(1)(a))`
        IF `consented`
        THEN `defence: you, or a person with lawful authority to do so for you, consented to it, expressly or impliedly (cl 8(1)(b))`
        IF `reasonably believed necessary for safety`
        THEN `defence: the defendant reasonably believed it was necessary to prevent or lessen a serious threat to the life, health or safety of a person (cl 8(1)(c))`
        IF `incidental to defence of persons or property` AND `proportionate necessary and reasonable`
        THEN `defence: it was incidental to the exercise of a lawful right of defence of persons or property, and proportionate, necessary and reasonable (cl 8(1)(d))`
        IF `was a publication under defamation law` AND `would establish a related defence`
        THEN `defence: it was a publication, and the defendant would establish a defamation-law defence of absolute privilege, publication of public documents, or fair report of proceedings of public concern (cl 8(2), (3))`
        OTHERWISE `this clause gives no defence on these facts (cl 8, the text's no)`

§§ `Schedule 2 cl 20 and the whole -- Can I sue for a serious invasion of privacy?`

-- cl 20(1): "A person (including a personal representative of a deceased person) cannot assert, continue or enforce an
-- action under this Schedule in relation to: (a) an invasion of the privacy of a deceased person (whether occurring
-- before or after the deceased person's death); or (b) an invasion of privacy by a person who has died." A flat
-- negative: the text's no. (2), costs of discontinued proceedings, is for the court.
-- The whole question takes the bars in the order a claimant meets them: cl 20, then the Part 3 exemptions (cl 8A lets
-- the court decide them at any stage), then the cl 7 elements, then the cl 8 defences (fork F5).

DECLARE `Can I sue` IS ONE OF
    `no: an action cannot be asserted, continued or enforced about an invasion of the privacy of a person who has died (cl 20(1)(a), the text's no)`
    `no: an action cannot be asserted, continued or enforced about an invasion of privacy by a person who has died (cl 20(1)(b), the text's no)`
    `no: the Schedule does not apply` HAS `the exemption` IS AN `Is the invasion exempt`
    `no cause of action` HAS `the element` IS AN `Is there a cause of action`
    `you have a cause of action, but the defendant has a defence` HAS `the defence` IS A `Does the defendant have a defence`
    `yes: you have a cause of action in tort for a serious invasion of privacy, actionable without proof of damage (cl 7); you must start proceedings in time (cl 14)`

@export Can I sue for a serious invasion of my privacy under the statutory tort (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2)? Takes the bars in order: no action about a dead person's privacy or against a person who has died (cl 20), the exemptions (cls 15-18), the five elements of the cause of action (cl 7) and the defences (cl 8).
GIVEN `the person whose privacy was invaded has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are asking about the privacy of a person who has died (for example as their personal representative)
      `the invader has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who invaded your privacy has died
      `who` IS A `Who invaded my privacy` @desc Who invaded your privacy. One of: "a person or business not listed below", "a journalist", "the employer of a journalist, or a person engaging a journalist", "a person assisting a journalist, employed or engaged by the journalist's employer or by the person engaging the journalist", "a person assisting a journalist in that person's professional capacity", "a Commonwealth agency, or a State or Territory authority, that is not an intelligence agency or law enforcement body", "a staff member of such an agency or authority", "a law enforcement body", "a staff member of a law enforcement body", "an intelligence agency", "an ASIO affiliate, or an agent or staff member of an intelligence agency"
      `works professionally as a journalist` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where a journalist is involved: whether that journalist works in a professional capacity as a journalist
      `subject to journalists' standards or code` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where a journalist is involved: whether that journalist is subject to standards of professional conduct, or a code of practice, that apply to journalists
      `the material` IS A `What the material was` @desc What the material involved was. One of: "news, current affairs or a documentary", "commentary or opinion on, or analysis of, news, current affairs or a documentary", "editorial content relating to news, current affairs or a documentary", "none of these"
      `collecting preparing or publishing it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion involved collecting, preparing for publication or publishing that material
      `publishing or distributing a journalist's material` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion involved publishing or distributing that material after a journalist prepared it for publication
      `in good faith in a function or power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where an agency, authority or its staff member acted: whether it acted in good faith in performing (or purporting to perform) a function, or exercising (or purporting to exercise) a power, of the agency or authority
      `in the performance of duties or functions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where a staff member, agent or affiliate of a law enforcement body or intelligence agency acted: whether it was in the performance of their duties, powers or functions as such
      `disclosure to law enforcement or intelligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion involved disclosing information to a law enforcement body or an intelligence agency
      `information from law enforcement or intelligence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion involved information that a law enforcement body or an intelligence agency had disclosed
      `the invader was under 18` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who invaded your privacy was under 18 years of age
      `physically intruded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person physically intruded into your private space
      `watched listened or recorded` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person watched, listened to or recorded your private activities or private affairs
      `collected used or disclosed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person collected, used or disclosed information about you
      `court finds another intrusion or misuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that something else the person did intruded upon your seclusion or misused information about you (answer no if not yet decided)
      `reasonable expectation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that a person in your position would have had a reasonable expectation of privacy in all the circumstances
      `intentional or reckless` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion was intentional or reckless (reckless as in the Criminal Code: aware of a substantial risk and unjustifiably taking it)
      `serious` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the invasion of privacy was serious
      `public interest outweighs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the public interest in your privacy outweighed any countervailing public interest
      `required or authorised by law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion was required or authorised by or under an Australian law or a court or tribunal order
      `consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, or a person with lawful authority to do so for you, consented to the invasion, expressly or impliedly
      `reasonably believed necessary for safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant reasonably believed the invasion was necessary to prevent or lessen a serious threat to the life, health or safety of a person
      `incidental to defence of persons or property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion was incidental to the exercise of a lawful right of defence of persons or property
      `proportionate necessary and reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds that invasion was proportionate, necessary and reasonable
      `was a publication under defamation law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the invasion was publishing information about you, within the meaning of an Australian defamation law
      `would establish a related defence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant would establish, under that defamation law, a defence of absolute privilege, of publication of public documents, or of fair report of proceedings of public concern
GIVETH A `Can I sue`
`Schedule 2 -- can I sue` `the person whose privacy was invaded has died` `the invader has died` `who` `works professionally as a journalist` `subject to journalists' standards or code` `the material` `collecting preparing or publishing it` `publishing or distributing a journalist's material` `in good faith in a function or power` `in the performance of duties or functions` `disclosure to law enforcement or intelligence` `information from law enforcement or intelligence` `the invader was under 18` `physically intruded` `watched listened or recorded` `collected used or disclosed` `court finds another intrusion or misuse` `reasonable expectation` `intentional or reckless` `serious` `public interest outweighs` `required or authorised by law` `consented` `reasonably believed necessary for safety` `incidental to defence of persons or property` `proportionate necessary and reasonable` `was a publication under defamation law` `would establish a related defence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the person whose privacy was invaded has died`
        THEN `no: an action cannot be asserted, continued or enforced about an invasion of the privacy of a person who has died (cl 20(1)(a), the text's no)`
        IF `the invader has died`
        THEN `no: an action cannot be asserted, continued or enforced about an invasion of privacy by a person who has died (cl 20(1)(b), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT (`the exemption found` EQUALS `no exemption in Part 3 of Schedule 2 applies on these facts`))
        THEN `no: the Schedule does not apply` `the exemption found`
        IF (NOT (`the elements` EQUALS `yes: all five elements of cl 7(1) are met; the invasion is actionable without proof of damage (cl 7(2))`))
        THEN `no cause of action` `the elements`
        IF (NOT (`the defence found` EQUALS `this clause gives no defence on these facts (cl 8, the text's no)`))
        THEN `you have a cause of action, but the defendant has a defence` `the defence found`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you have a cause of action in tort for a serious invasion of privacy, actionable without proof of damage (cl 7); you must start proceedings in time (cl 14)`
    WHERE
        `the exemption found` MEANS `Part 3 -- is the invasion exempt` `who` `works professionally as a journalist` `subject to journalists' standards or code` `the material` `collecting preparing or publishing it` `publishing or distributing a journalist's material` `in good faith in a function or power` `in the performance of duties or functions` `disclosure to law enforcement or intelligence` `information from law enforcement or intelligence` `the invader was under 18`
        `the elements` MEANS `cl 7 -- do I have a cause of action` `physically intruded` `watched listened or recorded` `collected used or disclosed` `court finds another intrusion or misuse` `reasonable expectation` `intentional or reckless` `serious` `public interest outweighs`
        `the defence found` MEANS `cl 8 -- does the defendant have a defence` `required or authorised by law` `consented` `reasonably believed necessary for safety` `incidental to defence of persons or property` `proportionate necessary and reasonable` `was a publication under defamation law` `would establish a related defence`

§§ `Schedule 2 cls 19 and 14 -- By when must I start proceedings?`

-- cl 19(1): "This clause applies if: (a) a publisher publishes information that relates to an individual to the public
-- (the first publication); and (b) the publisher or an associate of the publisher subsequently publishes (whether or not
-- to the public) information that is substantially the same (the subsequent publication); and (c) the manner of the
-- subsequent publication is not materially different from the manner of the first publication." (2): "If an invasion of
-- the individual's privacy consists of the first publication or the subsequent publication, the invasion of privacy is
-- taken to occur on the day of the first publication for the purposes of this Schedule." (3): prominence and extent for
-- the court; (4): associate means an employee or contractor of the publisher, an associated entity (Corporations Act
-- 2001), or an employee or contractor of one. "Materially different" is the court's: an input.
@export On what day is an invasion of my privacy by a republication taken to have happened, for the statutory tort's time limits (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 19, the single publication rule)? A later publication of substantially the same information, by the publisher or an associate, in a manner not materially different, is taken to occur on the day of the first publication.
GIVEN `the invasion happened on` IS A DATE @desc The day the publication you complain of happened
      `a republication by the publisher or an associate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it repeated, substantially the same, information about you that the same publisher had first published to the public, and was published by that publisher or its employee, contractor, associated entity, or an employee or contractor of an associated entity
      `the manner is not materially different` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds the manner of the later publication is not materially different from the first (for example in prominence and extent)
      `the first publication was on` IS A DATE @desc The day the publisher first published the information to the public
GIVETH A DATE
`cl 19 -- the day the invasion is taken to occur` `the invasion happened on` `a republication by the publisher or an associate` `the manner is not materially different` `the first publication was on` MEANS
    IF `a republication by the publisher or an associate` AND `the manner is not materially different`
    THEN `the first publication was on`
    ELSE `the invasion happened on`

-- cl 14(1): "The plaintiff must commence proceedings under this Schedule: (a) if the plaintiff was under 18 years of age
-- when the invasion of privacy occurred--before the plaintiff's 21st birthday; or (b) otherwise--before the earlier of:
-- (i) the day that is 1 year after the day on which the plaintiff became aware of the invasion of privacy; and (ii) the
-- day that is 3 years after the invasion of privacy occurred."
-- (2): the plaintiff "may apply to the court for an order that, despite subclause (1), the plaintiff may commence
-- proceedings under this Schedule before a day specified in the order." (3): "The court may make the order if the court
-- is satisfied that it was not reasonable in the circumstances for the plaintiff to have commenced proceedings in
-- accordance with subclause (1)". (4): "The day specified in the order must not be later than 6 years after the day on
-- which the invasion of privacy occurred."
-- "Before" a day: the last day to commence is the day before it. A person is under 18 until their 18th birthday; a
-- birthday on 29 February falls on 28 February in a common year (fork F1). Awareness before the invasion is counted as
-- awareness on the day of the invasion.

GIVEN `born on` IS A DATE, `the invasion occurred on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `cl 14(1)(a) -- was I under 18 when it happened` `born on` `the invasion occurred on` IF
    `the invasion occurred on` `is earlier than` (`born on` `plus calendar years` 18)

@export What is the last day on which I can start proceedings for a serious invasion of my privacy without a court order (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 14(1))? If I was under 18 when it happened, before my 21st birthday; otherwise before the earlier of 1 year after I became aware of it and 3 years after it happened.
GIVEN `born on` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `the invasion occurred on` IS A DATE @desc The day the invasion of your privacy occurred (for a republication, the day the single publication rule gives)
      `became aware on` IS A DATE @desc The day you became aware of the invasion of your privacy
GIVETH A DATE
`cl 14(1) -- the last day to start proceedings` `born on` `the invasion occurred on` `became aware on` MEANS
    IF `cl 14(1)(a) -- was I under 18 when it happened` `born on` `the invasion occurred on`
    THEN `the day before` (`born on` `plus calendar years` 21)
    ELSE `the day before` (`the earlier of` (`the awareness day` `plus calendar years` 1) `and` (`the invasion occurred on` `plus calendar years` 3))
    WHERE
        `the awareness day` MEANS IF `became aware on` `is earlier than` `the invasion occurred on` THEN `the invasion occurred on` ELSE `became aware on`

@export What is the latest day a court could let me start proceedings for a serious invasion of my privacy, if it extends the time (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 14(2)-(4))? The day in the court's order must not be later than 6 years after the invasion, and proceedings must be started before that day.
GIVEN `the invasion occurred on` IS A DATE @desc The day the invasion of your privacy occurred (for a republication, the day the single publication rule gives)
GIVETH A DATE
`cl 14(4) -- the last day to start under the longest order` `the invasion occurred on` MEANS `the day before` (`the invasion occurred on` `plus calendar years` 6)

DECLARE `Plaintiff` IS ONE OF `the plaintiff`
DECLARE `Plaintiff step` IS ONE OF `commence proceedings under Schedule 2`

-- cl 14 as a duty with a deadline, counted in days from the day of the invasion (day 0); the last day allowed is the day
-- before the cl 14(1) day, or before the day in the court's order.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Plaintiff` `Plaintiff step`
`cl 14 -- commence proceedings before the day` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the plaintiff`
    MUST `commence proceedings under Schedule 2`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the plaintiff` BECAUSE "proceedings not commenced before the day cl 14 of Schedule 2 allows: out of time unless the court orders a later day (cl 14(2)-(4))"

-- Runs the rule over what happened. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH, so
-- FULFILLED means in time and anything else late. Not exported; it takes no DEONTIC from outside.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `cl 14 -- commencing on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`cl 14 -- commence proceedings before the day` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the plaintiff` `commence proceedings under Schedule 2` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Are my proceedings in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: started before the day cl 14(1) of Schedule 2 sets`
    `out of time under cl 14(1), but in time under the court's order extending the time (cl 14(2), (3))`
    `out of time: not started before the day cl 14(1) sets, and there is no court order extending the time; the court may make one if satisfied it was not reasonable for you to start in time, for a day not later than 6 years after the invasion (cl 14, the text's no)`
    `out of time: not started before the day cl 14(1) sets, nor before the day in the court's order (read as no later than 6 years after the invasion) (cl 14, the text's no)`

@export Are my proceedings for a serious invasion of my privacy in time (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 14)? They must be started before my 21st birthday if I was under 18 when it happened, otherwise before the earlier of 1 year after I became aware of it and 3 years after it happened, unless the court has ordered a later day (no later than 6 years after it happened).
GIVEN `born on` IS A DATE @desc Your date of birth
      `the invasion occurred on` IS A DATE @desc The day the invasion of your privacy occurred (for a republication, the day the single publication rule gives)
      `became aware on` IS A DATE @desc The day you became aware of the invasion of your privacy
      `started on` IS A DATE @desc The day you started (or will start) the proceedings
      `the court made an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court has ordered that you may start proceedings before a later day (because it was satisfied it was not reasonable for you to start in time)
      `the day in the order` IS A DATE @desc If the court made such an order, the day specified in it (otherwise any date)
GIVETH AN `Are my proceedings in time`
`cl 14 -- are my proceedings in time` `born on` `the invasion occurred on` `became aware on` `started on` `the court made an order` `the day in the order` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `cl 14 -- commencing on this day is in time` (`the day number of` (`cl 14(1) -- the last day to start proceedings` `born on` `the invasion occurred on` `became aware on`) `counted from` `the invasion occurred on`) `the day started`
        THEN `in time: started before the day cl 14(1) of Schedule 2 sets`
        IF (NOT `the court made an order`)
        THEN `out of time: not started before the day cl 14(1) sets, and there is no court order extending the time; the court may make one if satisfied it was not reasonable for you to start in time, for a day not later than 6 years after the invasion (cl 14, the text's no)`
        IF `cl 14 -- commencing on this day is in time` (`the day number of` (`the day before` `the order's day as allowed`) `counted from` `the invasion occurred on`) `the day started`
        THEN `out of time under cl 14(1), but in time under the court's order extending the time (cl 14(2), (3))`
        OTHERWISE `out of time: not started before the day cl 14(1) sets, nor before the day in the court's order (read as no later than 6 years after the invasion) (cl 14, the text's no)`
    WHERE
        `the day started` MEANS `the day number of` `started on` `counted from` `the invasion occurred on`
        `the six-year day` MEANS `the invasion occurred on` `plus calendar years` 6
        `the order's day as allowed` MEANS `the earlier of` `the day in the order` `and` `the six-year day`

§§ `Schedule 2 cl 10 -- Can my claim be ended early?`

-- cl 10(1): "The court may give judgment for the defendant if the court is satisfied that the plaintiff has no reasonable
-- prospect of successfully prosecuting the proceedings." A discretion on the court's satisfaction: an input.
DECLARE `Can my claim be ended early` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court may give judgment for the defendant, since it is satisfied you have no reasonable prospect of success (cl 10(1))`
    `not under cl 10: the court is not satisfied you have no reasonable prospect of success (NOTHING-kind; the court's other powers remain, cl 10(2))`

@export Can the court end my statutory privacy claim early by giving judgment for the defendant (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 10)? It may if it is satisfied I have no reasonable prospect of successfully prosecuting the proceedings.
GIVEN `court satisfied no reasonable prospect` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that you have no reasonable prospect of successfully prosecuting the proceedings
GIVETH A `Can my claim be ended early`
`cl 10 -- can my claim be ended early` `court satisfied no reasonable prospect` MEANS
    IF `court satisfied no reasonable prospect`
    THEN `yes: the court may give judgment for the defendant, since it is satisfied you have no reasonable prospect of success (cl 10(1))`
    ELSE `not under cl 10: the court is not satisfied you have no reasonable prospect of success (NOTHING-kind; the court's other powers remain, cl 10(2))`

§§ `Schedule 2 cls 11-13 -- What can I be awarded?`

-- cl 11: "(1) Subject to this clause, the court may award damages to the plaintiff. (2) The court must not award
-- aggravated damages. (3) The court may award damages for emotional distress. (4) The court may award exemplary or
-- punitive damages in exceptional circumstances. (5) The sum of: (a) any damages awarded for non-economic loss; and
-- (b) any exemplary or punitive damages; must not exceed the greater of: (c) $478,550; and (d) the maximum amount of
-- damages for non-economic loss that may be awarded in defamation proceedings under an Australian law."
-- (6): matters the court may consider in the amount (apology, correction, other compensation, steps to settle, the
-- defendant's later conduct): for the court, not encoded.
-- Damages for emotional distress are read as damages for non-economic loss, inside the (5) cap (fork F6).

DECLARE `Kind of damages` IS ONE OF
    `damages for economic loss`
    `damages for non-economic loss`
    `damages for emotional distress`
    `aggravated damages`
    `exemplary or punitive damages`

DECLARE `Can I be awarded this kind of damages` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court may award damages (cl 11(1)); damages for economic loss are outside the cap in cl 11(5)`
    `yes: the court may award damages for non-economic loss (cl 11(1)), within the cap in cl 11(5)`
    `yes: the court may award damages for emotional distress (cl 11(3)), counted as non-economic loss within the cap in cl 11(5)`
    `no: the court must not award aggravated damages (cl 11(2), the text's no)`
    `yes: in exceptional circumstances the court may award exemplary or punitive damages (cl 11(4)), within the cap in cl 11(5)`
    `not in these circumstances: cl 11(4) gives exemplary or punitive damages in exceptional circumstances, and the court has not found them (NOTHING-kind)`

@export Can I be awarded this kind of damages for a serious invasion of my privacy (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 11)? Damages generally, and for emotional distress, may be awarded; aggravated damages must not; exemplary or punitive damages only in exceptional circumstances.
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of damages` @desc The kind of damages. One of: "damages for economic loss", "damages for non-economic loss", "damages for emotional distress", "aggravated damages", "exemplary or punitive damages"
      `exceptional circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court finds there are exceptional circumstances (relevant only to exemplary or punitive damages)
GIVETH A `Can I be awarded this kind of damages`
`cl 11 -- can I be awarded this kind of damages` `the kind` `exceptional circumstances` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `damages for economic loss` THEN `yes: the court may award damages (cl 11(1)); damages for economic loss are outside the cap in cl 11(5)`
    WHEN `damages for non-economic loss` THEN `yes: the court may award damages for non-economic loss (cl 11(1)), within the cap in cl 11(5)`
    WHEN `damages for emotional distress` THEN `yes: the court may award damages for emotional distress (cl 11(3)), counted as non-economic loss within the cap in cl 11(5)`
    WHEN `aggravated damages` THEN `no: the court must not award aggravated damages (cl 11(2), the text's no)`
    WHEN `exemplary or punitive damages` THEN
        IF `exceptional circumstances`
        THEN `yes: in exceptional circumstances the court may award exemplary or punitive damages (cl 11(4)), within the cap in cl 11(5)`
        ELSE `not in these circumstances: cl 11(4) gives exemplary or punitive damages in exceptional circumstances, and the court has not found them (NOTHING-kind)`

`cl 11(5)(c) -- the fixed cap` MEANS 478550

@export What is the most I can be awarded for non-economic loss and exemplary or punitive damages together, for a serious invasion of my privacy (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 11(5))? The greater of $478,550 and the maximum damages for non-economic loss that may be awarded in defamation proceedings under an Australian law.
GIVEN `the defamation maximum` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum amount of damages for non-economic loss that may be awarded in defamation proceedings under an Australian law at the time, in dollars (0 if not known)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`cl 11(5) -- the cap` `the defamation maximum` MEANS
    IF `the defamation maximum` GREATER THAN `cl 11(5)(c) -- the fixed cap`
    THEN `the defamation maximum`
    ELSE `cl 11(5)(c) -- the fixed cap`

@export How much of the non-economic loss and exemplary or punitive damages I claim for a serious invasion of my privacy can be awarded, after the cap (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 11(5))? Their sum must not exceed the greater of $478,550 and the defamation maximum for non-economic loss.
GIVEN `non-economic loss damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The damages for non-economic loss (including emotional distress) the court would otherwise award, in dollars
      `exemplary or punitive damages` IS A NUMBER @desc The exemplary or punitive damages the court would otherwise award, in dollars (0 if none)
      `the defamation maximum` IS A NUMBER @desc The maximum amount of damages for non-economic loss that may be awarded in defamation proceedings under an Australian law at the time, in dollars (0 if not known)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`cl 11(5) -- the capped sum` `non-economic loss damages` `exemplary or punitive damages` `the defamation maximum` MEANS
    IF `the sum claimed` GREATER THAN `the cap`
    THEN `the cap`
    ELSE `the sum claimed`
    WHERE
        `the sum claimed` MEANS `non-economic loss damages` PLUS `exemplary or punitive damages`
        `the cap` MEANS `cl 11(5) -- the cap` `the defamation maximum`

-- cl 12(1): "The court may grant such remedies, in addition to or instead of damages awarded in accordance with clause
-- 11, as the court thinks most appropriate in the circumstances." (2): "Without limiting subclause (1), those remedies may
-- include one or more of the following: (a) an account of profits; (b) an injunction; (c) an order requiring the
-- defendant to apologise to the plaintiff; (d) a correction order; (e) an order that any material (including copies):
-- (i) that is in the defendant's possession, or that the defendant is able to retrieve; and (ii) that was obtained or
-- made as a result of the invasion of privacy or was misused during the course of the invasion of privacy; be destroyed,
-- be delivered up to the plaintiff or be dealt with as the court directs; (f) a declaration that the defendant has
-- seriously invaded the plaintiff's privacy." cl 9(1): an injunction "at any stage of the proceedings". The list is
-- open ("without limiting"): the last item says so.
DECLARE `Other remedy` IS ONE OF
    `an account of profits (cl 12(2)(a))`
    `an injunction, which may be granted at any stage of the proceedings (cl 12(2)(b), cl 9(1))`
    `an order that the defendant apologise to you (cl 12(2)(c))`
    `a correction order (cl 12(2)(d))`
    `an order that the material obtained, made or misused in the invasion be destroyed, delivered up to you or dealt with as the court directs (cl 12(2)(e))`
    `a declaration that the defendant has seriously invaded your privacy (cl 12(2)(f))`
    `any other remedy the court thinks most appropriate, in addition to or instead of damages (cl 12(1))`

@export What can the court order besides damages for a serious invasion of my privacy (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 12)? An account of profits, an injunction, an apology, a correction, the destruction or delivery up of the material, a declaration, or any other remedy it thinks most appropriate.
GIVEN `the defendant holds the material` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether material obtained or made as a result of the invasion, or misused during it, is in the defendant's possession or the defendant can retrieve it
GIVETH A LIST OF `Other remedy`
`cl 12 -- what else can the court order` `the defendant holds the material` MEANS
    IF `the defendant holds the material`
    THEN LIST `an account of profits (cl 12(2)(a))`, `an injunction, which may be granted at any stage of the proceedings (cl 12(2)(b), cl 9(1))`, `an order that the defendant apologise to you (cl 12(2)(c))`, `a correction order (cl 12(2)(d))`, `an order that the material obtained, made or misused in the invasion be destroyed, delivered up to you or dealt with as the court directs (cl 12(2)(e))`, `a declaration that the defendant has seriously invaded your privacy (cl 12(2)(f))`, `any other remedy the court thinks most appropriate, in addition to or instead of damages (cl 12(1))`
    ELSE LIST `an account of profits (cl 12(2)(a))`, `an injunction, which may be granted at any stage of the proceedings (cl 12(2)(b), cl 9(1))`, `an order that the defendant apologise to you (cl 12(2)(c))`, `a correction order (cl 12(2)(d))`, `a declaration that the defendant has seriously invaded your privacy (cl 12(2)(f))`, `any other remedy the court thinks most appropriate, in addition to or instead of damages (cl 12(1))`

-- cl 13: "an apology made by or on behalf of the defendant in connection with the invasion of privacy: (a) does not
-- constitute an express or implied admission of fault or liability by the defendant in connection with the invasion of
-- privacy; and (b) is not relevant to the determination of fault or liability". The note points to cl 11(6)(a).
DECLARE `Does their apology admit fault` IS ONE OF
    `no: an apology is not an admission of fault or liability and is not relevant to deciding fault or liability (cl 13, the text's no); the court may consider it in setting damages (cl 11(6)(a))`
    `no apology has been made: cl 13 does not arise (NOTHING-kind)`

@export If the person who invaded my privacy apologises, does that count as admitting fault (Privacy Act 1988 Schedule 2 cl 13)? No: it is not an admission and is not relevant to fault or liability, though the court may consider it when setting damages.
GIVEN `they apologised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant, or someone on their behalf, apologised to you in connection with the invasion
GIVETH A `Does their apology admit fault`
`cl 13 -- does their apology admit fault` `they apologised` MEANS
    IF `they apologised`
    THEN `no: an apology is not an admission of fault or liability and is not relevant to deciding fault or liability (cl 13, the text's no); the court may consider it in setting damages (cl 11(6)(a))`
    ELSE `no apology has been made: cl 13 does not arise (NOTHING-kind)`

§§ `Part V ss 36, 38 -- Can I complain for a group (a representative complaint)?`

-- s 36(1): "An individual may complain to the Commissioner about an act or practice that may be an interference with the
-- privacy of the individual." (2): "In the case of an act or practice that may be an interference with the privacy of 2
-- or more individuals, any one of those individuals may make a complaint under subsection (1) on behalf of all of the
-- individuals." (3): "A complaint shall be in writing." (5): "The complaint shall specify the respondent".
-- s 38(1): "A representative complaint may be lodged under section 36 only if: (a) the class members have complaints
-- against the same person or entity; and (b) all the complaints are in respect of, or arise out of, the same, similar or
-- related circumstances; and (c) all the complaints give rise to a substantial common issue of law or fact." (2): "A
-- representative complaint made under section 36 must: (a) describe or otherwise identify the class members; and (b)
-- specify the nature of the complaints made on behalf of the class members; and (c) specify the nature of the relief
-- sought; and (d) specify the questions of law or fact that are common to the complaints of the class members. In
-- describing or otherwise identifying the class members, it is not necessary to name them or specify how many there
-- are." (3): "A representative complaint may be lodged without the consent of class members."
-- Whether an act "may be" an interference with privacy (s 13) is the baseline of October 2026's ground; here a fact.
-- A complaint not in writing, not naming the respondent, or missing a s 38(2) particular: "not as it stands" (fork F7).

DECLARE `Can I lodge a representative complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may complain to the Commissioner on behalf of all of them; you need not name them, count them or get their consent (ss 36(2), 38(2), (3))`
    `no: one individual may complain for others only about an act or practice that may interfere with the privacy of 2 or more individuals, including that individual (s 36(2), the text's no)`
    `no: a representative complaint may be lodged only if the class members have complaints against the same person or entity, arising out of the same, similar or related circumstances, and raising a substantial common issue of law or fact (s 38(1), the text's no)`
    `not as it stands: a complaint must be in writing and must specify the respondent (s 36(3), (5))`
    `not as it stands: a representative complaint must identify the class members and specify the nature of their complaints, the relief sought and the common questions of law or fact (s 38(2))`

@export Can I lodge a representative complaint with the Information Commissioner on behalf of a group whose privacy was interfered with (Privacy Act 1988 ss 36 and 38)? One of the individuals may complain for all of them, without their consent, if the complaints are against the same person, arise out of the same, similar or related circumstances and raise a substantial common issue.
GIVEN `affects 2 or more including me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the act or practice may be an interference with the privacy of 2 or more individuals, you among them
      `same respondent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the class members' complaints are against the same person or entity
      `same similar or related circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the complaints are about, or arise out of, the same, similar or related circumstances
      `substantial common issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the complaints give rise to a substantial common issue of law or fact
      `in writing naming the respondent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint is in writing and specifies the respondent
      `has the representative particulars` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint describes or identifies the class members (names and numbers are not needed) and specifies the nature of their complaints, the relief sought, and the common questions of law or fact
GIVETH A `Can I lodge a representative complaint`
`ss 36, 38 -- can I lodge a representative complaint` `affects 2 or more including me` `same respondent` `same similar or related circumstances` `substantial common issue` `in writing naming the respondent` `has the representative particulars` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `affects 2 or more including me`)
        THEN `no: one individual may complain for others only about an act or practice that may interfere with the privacy of 2 or more individuals, including that individual (s 36(2), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT (`same respondent` AND `same similar or related circumstances` AND `substantial common issue`))
        THEN `no: a representative complaint may be lodged only if the class members have complaints against the same person or entity, arising out of the same, similar or related circumstances, and raising a substantial common issue of law or fact (s 38(1), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `in writing naming the respondent`)
        THEN `not as it stands: a complaint must be in writing and must specify the respondent (s 36(3), (5))`
        IF (NOT `has the representative particulars`)
        THEN `not as it stands: a representative complaint must identify the class members and specify the nature of their complaints, the relief sought and the common questions of law or fact (s 38(2))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: you may complain to the Commissioner on behalf of all of them; you need not name them, count them or get their consent (ss 36(2), 38(2), (3))`

§§ `Part V ss 38B, 39 -- I am a class member: can I leave, or complain on my own?`

-- s 38B(2): "A class member may, by notice in writing to the Commissioner, withdraw from a representative complaint:
-- (a) if the complaint was lodged without the consent of the member--at any time; or (b) otherwise--at any time before
-- the Commissioner begins to hold an inquiry into the complaint." Note: a former member "may make a complaint under
-- section 36 that relates to the matter". A period the text fixes ("at any time before"): outside it, the text's no.
DECLARE `Can I withdraw from the representative complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes, at any time: it was lodged without your consent; give the Commissioner written notice (s 38B(2)(a)); you may then complain on your own (s 36, note to s 38B(2))`
    `yes: the Commissioner has not begun to hold an inquiry into it; give the Commissioner written notice (s 38B(2)(b)); you may then complain on your own (s 36, note to s 38B(2))`
    `no: you consented to it, and the Commissioner has begun to hold an inquiry into it (s 38B(2)(b), the text's no)`

@export Can I withdraw from a representative privacy complaint made on my behalf (Privacy Act 1988 s 38B(2))? At any time if it was lodged without my consent; otherwise only before the Commissioner begins to hold an inquiry. Withdrawal is by written notice to the Commissioner.
GIVEN `I consented to it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative complaint was lodged with your consent
      `the inquiry has begun` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has begun to hold an inquiry into the complaint
GIVETH A `Can I withdraw from the representative complaint`
`s 38B(2) -- can I withdraw from the representative complaint` `I consented to it` `the inquiry has begun` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `I consented to it`)
        THEN `yes, at any time: it was lodged without your consent; give the Commissioner written notice (s 38B(2)(a)); you may then complain on your own (s 36, note to s 38B(2))`
        IF (NOT `the inquiry has begun`)
        THEN `yes: the Commissioner has not begun to hold an inquiry into it; give the Commissioner written notice (s 38B(2)(b)); you may then complain on your own (s 36, note to s 38B(2))`
        OTHERWISE `no: you consented to it, and the Commissioner has begun to hold an inquiry into it (s 38B(2)(b), the text's no)`

-- s 39: "A person who is a class member for a representative complaint is not entitled to lodge a complaint in respect of
-- the same subject matter." A flat negative. Once the person has withdrawn, they are no longer a class member.
DECLARE `Can I lodge my own complaint` IS ONE OF
    `no: a class member of a representative complaint is not entitled to lodge a complaint about the same subject matter (s 39, the text's no); you may withdraw first (s 38B(2))`
    `yes: s 39 does not stop you; an individual may complain to the Commissioner about an act or practice that may be an interference with their privacy (s 36(1))`

@export Can I lodge my own privacy complaint while a representative complaint covers me (Privacy Act 1988 s 39)? Not about the same subject matter while I am a class member.
GIVEN `I am a class member` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a class member of a representative complaint (and have not withdrawn from it)
      `same subject matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your own complaint would be about the same subject matter as the representative complaint
GIVETH A `Can I lodge my own complaint`
`s 39 -- can I lodge my own complaint` `I am a class member` `same subject matter` MEANS
    IF `I am a class member` AND `same subject matter`
    THEN `no: a class member of a representative complaint is not entitled to lodge a complaint about the same subject matter (s 39, the text's no); you may withdraw first (s 38B(2))`
    ELSE `yes: s 39 does not stop you; an individual may complain to the Commissioner about an act or practice that may be an interference with their privacy (s 36(1))`

§§ `Part V s 52 -- What can the Commissioner's determination give me?`

-- s 52(1): "After investigating a complaint, the Commissioner may: (a) make a determination dismissing the complaint; or
-- (b) find the complaint substantiated and make a determination that includes one or more of the following: (i) a
-- declaration ... that the respondent has engaged in conduct constituting an interference with the privacy of an
-- individual and must not repeat or continue such conduct; (ia) a declaration that the respondent must take specified
-- steps within a specified period to ensure that such conduct is not repeated or continued; (ii) a declaration that the
-- respondent must perform any reasonable act or course of conduct to redress any loss or damage suffered ... by the
-- complainant; (iia) a declaration that the respondent must prepare and publish, or otherwise communicate, a statement
-- about the conduct (see section 52A); (iii) a declaration that the complainant is entitled to a specified amount by way
-- of compensation for any loss or damage suffered by reason of the act or practice the subject of the complaint; (iv) a
-- declaration that it would be inappropriate for any further action to be taken in the matter."
-- (1AB): loss or damage "includes: (a) injury to the feelings of the complainant or individual; and (b) humiliation".
-- (1B): "A determination ... is not binding or conclusive between any of the parties to the determination."
-- (3): "In a determination under paragraph (1)(a) or (b) (other than a determination made on a representative
-- complaint), the Commissioner may include a declaration that the complainant is entitled to a specified amount to
-- reimburse the complainant for expenses reasonably incurred ... in connection with the making of the complaint and the
-- investigation". (4): on a representative complaint, amounts may be specified or worked out in a specified manner.
-- "(other than ...)": on a representative complaint the reimbursement is left out (fork F8).
DECLARE `What a determination can give me` IS ONE OF
    `a declaration that the respondent interfered with privacy and must not repeat or continue the conduct (s 52(1)(b)(i))`
    `a declaration that the respondent must take specified steps within a specified period so the conduct is not repeated (s 52(1)(b)(ia))`
    `a declaration that the respondent must do any reasonable act or course of conduct to redress your loss or damage (s 52(1)(b)(ii))`
    `a declaration that the respondent must prepare and publish or communicate a statement about the conduct (s 52(1)(b)(iia), s 52A)`
    `a declaration that you are entitled to a specified amount of compensation, including for injury to feelings and humiliation (s 52(1)(b)(iii), (1AB))`
    `on a representative complaint, compensation of specified amounts or amounts worked out in a specified manner, with provision for paying the class members (s 52(4))`
    `a declaration that you are entitled to a specified amount to reimburse expenses reasonably incurred in making the complaint and in the investigation (s 52(3))`
    `the determination is not binding or conclusive between the parties (s 52(1B)); it is enforced by proceedings in court (ss 55A, 62)`

@export What can the Information Commissioner's determination on my privacy complaint give me if the complaint is substantiated (Privacy Act 1988 s 52)? Declarations that the conduct stop and be redressed, a published statement, compensation (including for injury to feelings and humiliation) and, except on a representative complaint, reimbursement of expenses.
GIVEN `a representative complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint is a representative complaint (made on behalf of a group of class members)
GIVETH A LIST OF `What a determination can give me`
`s 52 -- what a determination can give me` `a representative complaint` MEANS
    IF `a representative complaint`
    THEN LIST `a declaration that the respondent interfered with privacy and must not repeat or continue the conduct (s 52(1)(b)(i))`, `a declaration that the respondent must take specified steps within a specified period so the conduct is not repeated (s 52(1)(b)(ia))`, `a declaration that the respondent must do any reasonable act or course of conduct to redress your loss or damage (s 52(1)(b)(ii))`, `a declaration that the respondent must prepare and publish or communicate a statement about the conduct (s 52(1)(b)(iia), s 52A)`, `a declaration that you are entitled to a specified amount of compensation, including for injury to feelings and humiliation (s 52(1)(b)(iii), (1AB))`, `on a representative complaint, compensation of specified amounts or amounts worked out in a specified manner, with provision for paying the class members (s 52(4))`, `the determination is not binding or conclusive between the parties (s 52(1B)); it is enforced by proceedings in court (ss 55A, 62)`
    ELSE LIST `a declaration that the respondent interfered with privacy and must not repeat or continue the conduct (s 52(1)(b)(i))`, `a declaration that the respondent must take specified steps within a specified period so the conduct is not repeated (s 52(1)(b)(ia))`, `a declaration that the respondent must do any reasonable act or course of conduct to redress your loss or damage (s 52(1)(b)(ii))`, `a declaration that the respondent must prepare and publish or communicate a statement about the conduct (s 52(1)(b)(iia), s 52A)`, `a declaration that you are entitled to a specified amount of compensation, including for injury to feelings and humiliation (s 52(1)(b)(iii), (1AB))`, `a declaration that you are entitled to a specified amount to reimburse expenses reasonably incurred in making the complaint and in the investigation (s 52(3))`, `the determination is not binding or conclusive between the parties (s 52(1B)); it is enforced by proceedings in court (ss 55A, 62)`

§§ `Part V s 52A -- Must the respondent give me the statement, and by when?`

-- s 52A(1): "If a determination under section 52 includes a declaration mentioned in subparagraph 52(1)(b)(iia) ... the
-- respondent must, within 14 days after receiving the determination (or such longer period as the Commissioner allows):
-- (a) prepare a statement, in consultation with the Commissioner, setting out [who the respondent is, the conduct, the
-- steps taken or to be taken, other required information]; and (b) if required by the declaration--give a copy of the
-- statement to the complainant or, if the complaint is a representative complaint, to each class member identified as
-- affected by the determination, in the manner specified by the declaration".
-- "If required by the declaration" is a "where" condition: when unmet, the text's no. The 14 days count from the day the
-- respondent received the determination (day 0); the last day is that day plus 14, or plus the longer period allowed.
-- A period shorter than 14 days is read as 14 (the Commissioner may allow only a longer one).

GIVEN `the period in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 52A(1) -- the days allowed` `the period in days` MEANS IF `the period in days` GREATER THAN 14 THEN `the period in days` ELSE 14

DECLARE `Respondent` IS ONE OF `the respondent`
DECLARE `Respondent step` IS ONE OF `give the complainant a copy of the statement`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Respondent` `Respondent step`
`s 52A(1)(b) -- give me a copy of the statement in time` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the respondent`
    MUST `give the complainant a copy of the statement`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the respondent` BECAUSE "copy of the statement not given within 14 days after receiving the determination, or the longer period the Commissioner allowed (s 52A(1)(b))"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 52A(1)(b) -- giving on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 52A(1)(b) -- give me a copy of the statement in time` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the respondent` `give the complainant a copy of the statement` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Has the respondent given me the statement in time` IS ONE OF
    `no duty to give you a copy: the declaration does not require it (s 52A(1)(b), the text's no)`
    `given in time (s 52A(1)(b))`
    `given late: the copy was due within 14 days after the respondent received the determination, or the longer period allowed (s 52A(1)(b)); an organisation or small business operator must comply (s 55(d)), and you or the Commissioner may go to court to enforce the determination (s 55A)`
    `not yet given, and the period has not ended` HAS `the last day for the statement` IS A DATE
    `not yet given, and the period has ended: the respondent has not complied (s 52A(1)(b)); you or the Commissioner may go to court to enforce the determination (ss 55A, 62)`

@export Must the respondent give me a copy of the statement the Information Commissioner's determination requires, and has it done so in time (Privacy Act 1988 s 52A(1)(b))? Where the declaration requires it, the respondent must give it within 14 days after receiving the determination, or a longer period the Commissioner allows.
GIVEN `the declaration requires a copy for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the determination's declaration requires the respondent to give you (or, on a representative complaint, each affected class member) a copy of the statement
      `the respondent received it on` IS A DATE @desc The day the respondent received the determination
      `the period in days` IS A NUMBER @desc The period allowed, in days: 14, unless the Commissioner allowed a longer period
      `it has been given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the respondent has given you a copy of the statement
      `given on` IS A DATE @desc If given, the day it was given (otherwise any date)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `Has the respondent given me the statement in time`
`s 52A -- has the respondent given me the statement in time` `the declaration requires a copy for me` `the respondent received it on` `the period in days` `it has been given` `given on` `today` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `the declaration requires a copy for me`)
        THEN `no duty to give you a copy: the declaration does not require it (s 52A(1)(b), the text's no)`
        IF `it has been given` AND `s 52A(1)(b) -- giving on this day is in time` `the days allowed` (`the day number of` `given on` `counted from` `the respondent received it on`)
        THEN `given in time (s 52A(1)(b))`
        IF `it has been given`
        THEN `given late: the copy was due within 14 days after the respondent received the determination, or the longer period allowed (s 52A(1)(b)); an organisation or small business operator must comply (s 55(d)), and you or the Commissioner may go to court to enforce the determination (s 55A)`
        IF `the last day` `is earlier than` `today`
        THEN `not yet given, and the period has ended: the respondent has not complied (s 52A(1)(b)); you or the Commissioner may go to court to enforce the determination (ss 55A, 62)`
        OTHERWISE `not yet given, and the period has not ended` `the last day`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `s 52A(1) -- the days allowed` `the period in days`
        `the last day` MEANS `the respondent received it on` `plus days` `the days allowed`

§§ `Part V ss 55A, 62 -- Can I go to court to enforce the determination?`

-- s 55A(1): "The following persons may commence proceedings in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of
-- Australia (Division 2) for an order to enforce a determination: (a) if the determination was made under subsection
-- 52(1)--the complainant; (b) the Commissioner." (5): the court deals "by way of a hearing de novo" with whether there
-- was an interference. (8): on a representative complaint, "complainant ... means any of the class members".
-- s 54(1): Division 3 (s 55A) applies to a determination "except where the determination applies in relation to an
-- agency or the principal executive of an agency"; those go under s 62.
-- s 62(1): "If an agency fails to comply with section 58, an application may be made to the Federal Court or the Federal
-- Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) for an order directing the agency to comply." (2): the same for a
-- principal executive and s 59. (3): by "(a) if the determination was made under subsection 52(1)--the complainant; or
-- (b) the Commissioner." (5): "An application may not be made under this section in relation to a determination under
-- section 52 until: (a) the time has expired for making an application under section 96 for review of the
-- determination; or (b) if such an application is made, the decision of the Administrative Review Tribunal on the
-- application has come into operation." (6): complainant means a class member.
-- A determination after an own-motion investigation (s 52(1A)) leaves the complainant out of both lists; the lists are
-- not closed with "only": NOTHING-kind.
DECLARE `Can I enforce the determination` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you (or any class member) may start proceedings in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) to enforce it; the court decides afresh whether there was an interference with privacy (s 55A(1)(a), (5), (8))`
    `yes: the agency has not complied; you (or any class member) may apply to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) for an order directing it to comply (s 62(1)-(3), (6))`
    `not yet: no application may be made until the time for seeking Administrative Review Tribunal review has expired, or the Tribunal's decision on review has come into operation (s 62(5), the text's no)`
    `s 62 gives no application while the agency is complying with the determination (s 62(1), (2), the text's no)`
    `not you: the determination followed an investigation on the Commissioner's own initiative (s 52(1A)); ss 55A(1) and 62(3) name the complainant only for a determination on a complaint (NOTHING-kind); the Commissioner may enforce it`

@export Can I go to court to enforce the Information Commissioner's determination on my privacy complaint (Privacy Act 1988 ss 55A and 62)? Against an organisation or small business operator, I may start enforcement proceedings; against an agency, I may apply for an order that it comply once the time for tribunal review has passed.
GIVEN `made on my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the determination was made on your complaint (or a representative complaint you are a class member of), rather than after an investigation on the Commissioner's own initiative
      `against an agency` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the determination applies to a Commonwealth agency or the principal executive of an agency (rather than an organisation or small business operator)
      `the agency has not complied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where it applies to an agency: whether the agency or its principal executive has failed to comply with the determination
      `review time over or decided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where it applies to an agency: whether the time to apply to the Administrative Review Tribunal for review of the determination has expired, or the Tribunal's decision on such an application has come into operation
GIVETH A `Can I enforce the determination`
`ss 55A, 62 -- can I enforce the determination` `made on my complaint` `against an agency` `the agency has not complied` `review time over or decided` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `made on my complaint`)
        THEN `not you: the determination followed an investigation on the Commissioner's own initiative (s 52(1A)); ss 55A(1) and 62(3) name the complainant only for a determination on a complaint (NOTHING-kind); the Commissioner may enforce it`
        IF (NOT `against an agency`)
        THEN `yes: you (or any class member) may start proceedings in the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) to enforce it; the court decides afresh whether there was an interference with privacy (s 55A(1)(a), (5), (8))`
        IF (NOT `the agency has not complied`)
        THEN `s 62 gives no application while the agency is complying with the determination (s 62(1), (2), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `review time over or decided`)
        THEN `not yet: no application may be made until the time for seeking Administrative Review Tribunal review has expired, or the Tribunal's decision on review has come into operation (s 62(5), the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the agency has not complied; you (or any class member) may apply to the Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) for an order directing it to comply (s 62(1)-(3), (6))`

§§ `Part IIIA ss 25, 25A -- Can I get compensation for a credit reporting contravention?`

-- s 25(1): "The Federal Court or the Federal Circuit and Family Court of Australia (Division 2) may order an entity to
-- compensate a person for loss or damage (including injury to the person's feelings or humiliation) suffered by the
-- person if: (a) either: (i) a civil penalty order has been made under subsection 82(3) of the Regulatory Powers Act
-- against the entity for a contravention of a civil penalty provision of this Part; or (ii) the entity is found guilty
-- of an offence against this Part; and (b) that loss or damage resulted from the contravention or commission of the
-- offence." (2): "The court may make the order only if: (a) the person applies for an order under this section; and
-- (b) the application is made within 6 years of the day the cause of action that relates to the contravention or
-- commission of the offence accrued." (3): the amount "may be recovered as a debt due to the person".
-- s 25A(1)-(5): the same gateway for "such order as the Court considers appropriate" to compensate, or to prevent or
-- reduce loss "suffered, or likely to be suffered", with the same 6 years ((4)(b)).
-- "Within 6 years of the day": the last day is the sixth anniversary of that day (fork F9).

@export What is the last day on which I can apply to the court for compensation for a credit reporting contravention (Privacy Act 1988 ss 25(2)(b) and 25A(4)(b))? Within 6 years of the day the cause of action accrued.
GIVEN `the cause of action accrued on` IS A DATE @desc The day the cause of action relating to the contravention or offence accrued
GIVETH A DATE
`ss 25, 25A -- the last day to apply` `the cause of action accrued on` MEANS `the cause of action accrued on` `plus calendar years` 6

DECLARE `Applicant for compensation` IS ONE OF `the person who suffered loss`
DECLARE `Compensation step` IS ONE OF `apply to the court for a compensation order`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Applicant for compensation` `Compensation step`
`ss 25(2), 25A(4) -- apply within 6 years` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the person who suffered loss`
    MUST `apply to the court for a compensation order`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the person who suffered loss` BECAUSE "application not made within 6 years of the day the cause of action accrued: the court may not make the order (ss 25(2)(b), 25A(4)(b))"

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 25, 25A -- applying on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`ss 25(2), 25A(4) -- apply within 6 years` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the person who suffered loss` `apply to the court for a compensation order` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Can I get a compensation order` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the court may order the entity to compensate you, including for injury to feelings or humiliation, or make another order to compensate or to prevent or reduce the loss; an amount ordered is a debt due to you (ss 25(1), (3), 25A(2), (5))`
    `no: the court may make these orders only after a civil penalty order against the entity, or a finding of guilt of an offence, under Part IIIA (ss 25(1)(a), 25A(1)(a), the text's no)`
    `no: the loss or damage must have resulted from the contravention or the offence (ss 25(1)(b), 25A(1)(b), the text's no)`
    `no: the application must be made within 6 years of the day the cause of action accrued (ss 25(2)(b), 25A(4)(b), the text's no)`

@export Can I get a court order for compensation for loss caused by a credit reporting contravention (Privacy Act 1988 ss 25 and 25A)? Only after a civil penalty order or a finding of guilt under Part IIIA, for loss that resulted from it, on an application made within 6 years of the day the cause of action accrued.
GIVEN `penalty order or conviction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a civil penalty order has been made against the entity for contravening a civil penalty provision of Part IIIA (credit reporting), or the entity has been found guilty of an offence against Part IIIA
      `my loss resulted from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your loss or damage (including injury to feelings or humiliation) resulted from that contravention or offence, or is likely to
      `the cause of action accrued on` IS A DATE @desc The day the cause of action relating to the contravention or offence accrued
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The day you applied (or will apply) to the court
GIVETH A `Can I get a compensation order`
`ss 25, 25A -- can I get a compensation order` `penalty order or conviction` `my loss resulted from it` `the cause of action accrued on` `applied on` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `penalty order or conviction`)
        THEN `no: the court may make these orders only after a civil penalty order against the entity, or a finding of guilt of an offence, under Part IIIA (ss 25(1)(a), 25A(1)(a), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `my loss resulted from it`)
        THEN `no: the loss or damage must have resulted from the contravention or the offence (ss 25(1)(b), 25A(1)(b), the text's no)`
        IF (NOT (`ss 25, 25A -- applying on this day is in time` `the days allowed` `the day applied`))
        THEN `no: the application must be made within 6 years of the day the cause of action accrued (ss 25(2)(b), 25A(4)(b), the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the court may order the entity to compensate you, including for injury to feelings or humiliation, or make another order to compensate or to prevent or reduce the loss; an amount ordered is a debt due to you (ss 25(1), (3), 25A(2), (5))`
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the day number of` (`ss 25, 25A -- the last day to apply` `the cause of action accrued on`) `counted from` `the cause of action accrued on`
        `the day applied` MEANS `the day number of` `applied on` `counted from` `the cause of action accrued on`
